While it measures a box’s intrinsic inline sizes, Ladybird transfers
the box’s min and max block sizes to its inline axis without resolving
an intrinsic max-width, and applies that max-width after the transferred
limits instead. The other engines don’t consult max-width in the
transfer at all: Blink’s ComputeTransferredMinMaxInlineSizes(), WebKit’s
RenderBox::computeMinMaxLogicalWidthFromAspectRatio(), and the transfer
in Gecko’s nsIFrame::ComputeSize() work from the block-axis sizes alone.
Each applies the transferred limits before the explicit min and max
sizes, which caps the transferred minimum just the same.
